  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 321 AM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nSurface high pressure will build southeast from south central \nCanada into the eastern Dakotas and Minnesota Monday into Monday\nNight. At the same time, a mid level trough of low pressure \nwill cross the Rockies, emerging onto the high plains of \nColorado and Kansas by Tuesday morning. Increasing lift in \nadvance of this feature, will lead to an increased threat for \nlight precipitation across the high plains Monday night into \nTuesday. The latest deterministic GFS solution tonight has \ntrended precipitation further south compared to last night's \nruns. This trend has shown up in the NBM ensemble and NBM \ngridded forecast tonight. The latest NBM ensemble forecast \ntonight only has a 20 percent chance of reaching 0.01\" of QPF \n(12 hr. ending at noon Tuesday) over SW Nebraska. These \npercentages were 30 to 40% with last night's 01z NBM ensemble \nrun. The NBM forecast tonight for Monday night through Tuesday \nnow has only slight chance pops for the SW forecast area and \nthis seems reasonable. With the high pressure building into the \nDakotas and Minnesota, cold air will remain in place across the \nFA through Tuesday. Beyond Tuesday, the mid range model \nsolutions have a low amplitude west northwest flow across the \ncentral Rockies and high plains midweek. The deterministic solns\nincluding the GFS and EC solns do drive a northern stream \nshortwave trough across the Dakotas into the upper midwest late \nnext week. ATTM, the EC soln is more amplified with this feature\nand actually develops some light precipitation in eastern \nNebraska Friday with the GFS predominately dry. The latest \nensemble forecast from both the GFS and EC solns is \npredominately dry and will continue to message a dry forecast \nfor the end of next week. As for temperatures, they will begin \nto trend upward toward the middle of the week with 40s Wednesday\nand Thursday, transitioning to upper 40s to lower 50s for \nFriday. Based on the deterministic EC and GFS solns tonight, \nforecast highs Thursday and Friday may need to be increased \nupward if their forecast trends continue.",
